meta {
  name: Reconcile
  type: http
  seq: 5
}

post {
  url: {{groupService}}/reconcile
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "path": "{{groupPrefix}}{{streamName}}"
  }
}

tests {
  test("runs the roster of the group that path belongs to", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
    expect(res.body.prefix).to.equal(bru.getEnvVar("groupPrefix"));
  });
}

docs {
  # Reconcile

  Runs a group's roster against the relay again, named by a path rather than by a key.

  This is what the relay's own read hook calls, which is why it takes no key: a starting read announces the path it is on, and the answer is a run against a roster somebody else stated.
  It grants nothing a key would have bought, and a group nobody stated a roster for answers `enforced: false` and is left alone.

  Doing it by hand is how a run is watched without waiting for a reader to arrive.
  The hook is `deploy/reconcile-on-read.sh`, wired in `pathDefaults.runOnRead`.

  A path belonging to no group is refused with 400, a bare stream name naming no roster.
}
